#!/usr/bin/python3
"""The producer of /var/marwanos/steam/appinfo/<appid>.tsv, read out of the
appinfo cache Valve's own client already keeps on this disk.

WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT THIS FILE, precisely. steamdl writes an appmanifest for
every game it downloads, and `StateFlags 4` in that manifest does NOT convince
Valve's client the game is installed. The client compares the manifest's
`buildid` against the branch's current buildid and ORs in UpdateRequired on a
mismatch -- so a manifest written with `buildid 0`, which is what steamdl writes
when it has no sidecar to read, makes the client decide the game is out of date
and DOWNLOAD IT AGAIN, into the directory we were trying to own. That is ADR
0010's trap 3 and SteamRE issue #274. The second cost is quieter and just as
real: `config.installdir` is the directory name the client resolves a game's
files under, and without it steamdl guesses one from the title -- when the guess
is wrong the client finds no files where its own metadata says they are, decides
the game is not installed, and re-downloads it BESIDE ours, leaving several
gigabytes on the disk that nothing will ever read.

So: no sidecar, and every install on this machine downloads twice.

WHY THIS SOURCE AND NOT A WEB API. Measured 2026-08-12, so nobody has to
rediscover it:

  * DepotDownloader prints depot ids, manifest ids and `Total bytes on disk`,
    and never a buildid. It resolves one internally and keeps it.
  * ISteamApps/UpToDateCheck answers only for dedicated-server apps -- 480
    answers, 620 and 271590 return "Couldn't get app info for the app
    specified". It is not a buildid source.
  * ISteamApps/GetAppList is gone (404). IStoreService/GetAppList needs a Web
    API key this appliance does not have and should not carry.
  * store.steampowered.com/api/appdetails works without a key and gives `name`
    and `type` -- not buildid, and not installdir.

What is left is PICS appinfo over Steam's CM protocol, which is a protocol
implementation and a second network identity, or the cache the hidden client
maintains as a side effect of running. This appliance ships that client
precisely so a Steam-DRM'd game can start (ADR 0010), which means the cache is
always there. It is local, needs no network, no API key and no protobuf.

WHY PYTHON. appinfo.vdf is binary KeyValues with a length-prefixed app index
and, since June 2024, a deduplicated string table addressed by 32-bit indices.
Binary parsing in bash would be a mistake and a slow one; python3 arrives from
the ublue base and protocodec already established it as this feature's helper
language. No third-party imports, for the same reason protocodec has none.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO.

  * It does not GUESS. A wrong buildid is worse than an absent one, because
    absent is visible -- steamdl warns by name at every install commit and once
    at startup -- while wrong is silent and produces exactly the re-download
    this file exists to prevent. Every row it cannot read from the cache is
    OMITTED, and a file it cannot parse produces NO sidecar rather than a
    partial one.
  * It does not write a sidecar with zero rows. An empty file would silence
    steamdl's "no appinfo cache" warning while telling it nothing.
  * It does not touch the client's cache, ever. Read-only, one shot, no lock.
  * It does not speak to the network, and it has no code that could.
  * It does not know about branches other than `public`. A beta branch is a
    thing this appliance has no UI for choosing, and inventing a default other
    than the one Valve uses would be a buildid for a build we did not download.
  * It does not walk every app in the cache. It answers about the appids it is
    asked about; the cache holds tens of thousands of them and writing a sidecar
    for each would be minutes of work for one number.

THE PARSE IS SELF-CHECKING, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT. Every app
entry declares its own byte length, so a KeyValues walk that does not land
EXACTLY on that boundary means this file's idea of the format is wrong for this
Steam version -- and the answer to that is no sidecar and a loud line, never a
number pulled out of a mis-parse. The app index is walked to its terminator for
the same reason before anything is extracted. Both checks run against the real
file on the real machine, which is the only place the format can actually be
confirmed.

The boundary check is STRUCTURAL, NOT A CHECKSUM, and the difference is worth
stating: it catches a wrong header layout or a wrong idea of what `size` counts
-- the systematic mistakes, which would miss the boundary on essentially every
entry -- while a single corrupted byte inside one blob can, and in testing did,
resync and still land on it. So each entry's own SHA-1 is checked as well and
REPORTED rather than enforced. Reported because this code has never seen a real
appinfo.vdf: it is not certain which of the two hash fields covers the binary
blob or over exactly what range, and a hard gate built on that guess would
refuse every app on every machine while looking like a Steam-side change. A
match therefore PROVES the parse for that entry and says so in the journal; a
non-match proves nothing and is left at info. The first bench run is what turns
this from a diagnostic into something that could be enforced -- grep the
journal for `blob sha1` to find out.

THE CLIENT MAY BE WRITING THIS FILE WHILE WE READ IT. It is read in ONE shot
into memory, from one file descriptor, and a torn read shows up as a parse
failure -- which is already the "we do not know" path. No sidecar is written
from a half-read cache.

  appinfo <appid> [<appid> ...]   write /var/marwanos/steam/appinfo/<id>.tsv
  appinfo --print <appid>         write nothing, print the rows on stdout
  appinfo --vdf PATH <appid>      read a cache from somewhere else (testing)
  appinfo --branch NAME <appid>   a branch other than `public`

Exit status is 0 when every appid asked about produced rows, 1 otherwise --
"1" meaning "we do not know", which every caller already degrades through.

DIAGNOSTICS GO TO STDERR WITH systemd PRIORITY PREFIXES, matching steamd and
steamdl: this runs as a child of a unit whose stderr is the journal, and a
message on stdout would be mistaken for data by `--print`'s caller.
"""

import hashlib
import os
import stat
import struct
import sys

TAG = "marwanos-steam-appinfo"

# The cache the hidden client keeps. The same path steamdl calls CLIENT_STEAM,
# and /var/home rather than /home because /home is an ostree symlink.
CLIENT_APPINFO = ("/var/home/player/.var/app/com.valvesoftware.Steam"
                  "/.local/share/Steam/appcache/appinfo.vdf")

# From docs/steam-client-contract.md. steamdl reads exactly this directory.
OUT_DIR = "/var/marwanos/steam/appinfo"

DEFAULT_BRANCH = "public"

# A real appinfo.vdf is tens of megabytes. The cap is not a tuning knob, it is
# a refusal to read an arbitrary-sized file into the memory of an appliance
# that is also downloading a game -- a cache this large is a broken cache.
MAX_VDF_BYTES = 512 * 1024 * 1024

# KeyValues nests four or five deep in practice. The limit exists so a torn or
# hostile blob cannot recurse this process to death.
MAX_DEPTH = 32

# The three magics this file will parse. Refusing an unknown one LOUDLY is the
# point: the format has changed three times and each change moved the header,
# so a parser that pressed on regardless would read a plausible-looking integer
# out of the wrong offset and call it a buildid. That is the one outcome worse
# than having no sidecar.
#
#   0x07564427  v39, ~2017
#   0x07564428  v40, Dec 2022 -- adds a second (binary) SHA-1 per app entry
#   0x07564429  v41, Jun 2024 -- adds a file-level string table, and KEYS in
#                                the KeyValues blobs become uint32 indices into
#                                it rather than inline NUL-terminated strings
MAGIC_V39 = 0x07564427
MAGIC_V40 = 0x07564428
MAGIC_V41 = 0x07564429

# Bytes between an app entry's `size` field and the start of its KeyValues
# blob: info_state(4) last_updated(4) pics_token(8) sha1(20) change_number(4),
# plus the binary sha1(20) from v40 on.
FIXED_AFTER_SIZE = {MAGIC_V39: 40, MAGIC_V40: 60, MAGIC_V41: 60}

# Binary KeyValues type bytes.
KV_OBJECT = 0x00
KV_STRING = 0x01
KV_INT32 = 0x02
KV_FLOAT32 = 0x03
KV_POINTER = 0x04
KV_WIDESTRING = 0x05
KV_COLOR = 0x06
KV_UINT64 = 0x07
KV_END = 0x08
KV_INT64 = 0x0A
KV_END_ALT = 0x0B


def info(message):
    print("<6>%s: %s" % (TAG, message), file=sys.stderr)


def warn(message):
    print("<4>%s: %s" % (TAG, message), file=sys.stderr)


def err(message):
    print("<3>%s: %s" % (TAG, message), file=sys.stderr)


class ParseError(Exception):
    """The bytes are not the file we think they are.

    Every raise site here means "stop and write nothing". It is never a reason
    to fall back to a default value, because the only values in question are
    the ones whose wrongness is invisible.
    """


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary KeyValues
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _cstring(data, pos, end):
    nul = data.find(b"\x00", pos, end)
    if nul < 0:
        raise ParseError("a string runs past the end of its entry")
    # `replace` rather than `strict`: a game name in a decade-old cache may be
    # cp1252 rather than UTF-8, and a mangled character in a label is not a
    # reason to refuse a buildid. Structural faults still raise.
    return data[pos:nul].decode("utf-8", "replace"), nul + 1


def _wstring(data, pos, end):
    i = pos
    while True:
        if i + 2 > end:
            raise ParseError("a wide string runs past the end of its entry")
        if data[i] == 0 and data[i + 1] == 0:
            break
        i += 2
    return data[pos:i].decode("utf-16-le", "replace"), i + 2


def _read_object(data, pos, end, strings, depth, top=False):
    """One KeyValues object, returned as a dict, plus the position after it.

    KEYS ARE LOWERCASED. Valve writes them lowercase already; normalising means
    a future capitalisation cannot turn a found buildid into a missing one.
    Values are untouched.

    `top` marks the outermost call, where running out of bytes is a clean end
    rather than a fault. That is not laxity: the blob's own terminator layout
    (one END closing `appinfo`, one closing the document) has varied, and the
    caller's exact-boundary check below is what actually validates the parse.
    """
    if depth > MAX_DEPTH:
        raise ParseError("keyvalues nested more than %d deep" % MAX_DEPTH)

    out = {}
    while True:
        if pos >= end:
            if top:
                return out, pos
            raise ParseError("keyvalues ran off the end of the entry")

        vtype = data[pos]
        pos += 1
        if vtype in (KV_END, KV_END_ALT):
            return out, pos

        if strings is not None:
            if pos + 4 > end:
                raise ParseError("truncated key index")
            (index,) = struct.unpack_from("<I", data, pos)
            pos += 4
            if index >= len(strings):
                raise ParseError("key index %d is outside the string table" % index)
            key = strings[index]
        else:
            key, pos = _cstring(data, pos, end)

        if vtype == KV_OBJECT:
            value, pos = _read_object(data, pos, end, strings, depth + 1)
        elif vtype == KV_STRING:
            value, pos = _cstring(data, pos, end)
        elif vtype == KV_WIDESTRING:
            value, pos = _wstring(data, pos, end)
        elif vtype in (KV_INT32, KV_POINTER, KV_COLOR):
            if pos + 4 > end:
                raise ParseError("truncated 32-bit value")
            (value,) = struct.unpack_from("<i", data, pos)
            pos += 4
        elif vtype == KV_FLOAT32:
            if pos + 4 > end:
                raise ParseError("truncated float")
            (value,) = struct.unpack_from("<f", data, pos)
            pos += 4
        elif vtype == KV_UINT64:
            if pos + 8 > end:
                raise ParseError("truncated 64-bit value")
            (value,) = struct.unpack_from("<Q", data, pos)
            pos += 8
        elif vtype == KV_INT64:
            if pos + 8 > end:
                raise ParseError("truncated 64-bit value")
            (value,) = struct.unpack_from("<q", data, pos)
            pos += 8
        else:
            # Not a wire type this format has ever had. Everything after it
            # would be read at the wrong offset, which is how a parser starts
            # inventing numbers.
            raise ParseError("unknown keyvalues type 0x%02x" % vtype)

        out[key.lower()] = value


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _string_table(data, offset):
    if offset < 0 or offset + 4 > len(data):
        raise ParseError("the string table offset points outside the file")
    (count,) = struct.unpack_from("<I", data, offset)
    if count > 20000000:
        raise ParseError("the string table claims %d strings" % count)
    pos = offset + 4
    end = len(data)
    strings = []
    for _ in range(count):
        text, pos = _cstring(data, pos, end)
        strings.append(text)
    return strings


def _index(data):
    """Walk the app index, WITHOUT parsing any KeyValues.

    Returns (magic, strings, {appid: (blob_start, entry_end, hashes)}), where
    `hashes` is the entry's own SHA-1 fields -- the text one, and from v40 the
    binary one. They are carried rather than skipped so the extraction below
    can say whether the blob it walked hashes to something the file already
    claims about it. See the module docstring for why that is reported and not
    enforced.

    THE WALK IS THE FORMAT CHECK. Each entry is skipped by its own declared
    size, so if this file's idea of the header layout or of what `size` counts
    were wrong, the very next appid would be read out of the middle of a blob
    and the walk would either run off the end or hit an implausible size. It
    has to reach the terminating zero appid exactly. Only then is anything
    extracted -- which means a Steam-side format change is a refusal with a
    message, not a sidecar full of garbage.
    """
    if len(data) < 8:
        raise ParseError("shorter than a header")

    magic, _universe = struct.unpack_from("<II", data, 0)
    if magic not in FIXED_AFTER_SIZE:
        raise ParseError("unsupported appinfo.vdf magic 0x%08x" % magic)

    pos = 8
    strings = None
    limit = len(data)
    if magic == MAGIC_V41:
        if len(data) < 16:
            raise ParseError("shorter than a v41 header")
        (table_offset,) = struct.unpack_from("<q", data, 8)
        pos = 16
        strings = _string_table(data, table_offset)
        # The app index ends where the string table begins; nothing in the
        # index may reach into it.
        limit = table_offset

    fixed = FIXED_AFTER_SIZE[magic]
    apps = {}
    while True:
        if pos + 4 > limit:
            raise ParseError("the app index ends without a terminator")
        (appid,) = struct.unpack_from("<I", data, pos)
        if appid == 0:
            return magic, strings, apps
        if pos + 8 > limit:
            raise ParseError("an app entry is truncated")
        (size,) = struct.unpack_from("<I", data, pos + 4)
        entry_end = pos + 8 + size
        if size < fixed or entry_end > limit:
            raise ParseError("app %d declares an impossible size" % appid)
        body = pos + 8
        hashes = [bytes(data[body + 16:body + 36])]
        if magic != MAGIC_V39:
            hashes.append(bytes(data[body + 40:body + 60]))
        apps[appid] = (body + fixed, entry_end, tuple(hashes))
        pos = entry_end


def _blob_sha1(data, blob_start, entry_end, hashes):
    """Which of the entry's own SHA-1 fields, if either, covers this blob.

    Returns a short word for the journal. `text` and `binary` name the field
    that matched -- either is proof that the blob's start and end are exactly
    where this parser thinks they are, which is the one fact about this format
    that cannot be established anywhere but on a machine with a real cache.
    `none` means only that we do not know which range Valve hashes.
    """
    digest = hashlib.sha1(data[blob_start:entry_end]).digest()
    names = ("text", "binary")
    for name, known in zip(names, hashes):
        if known and digest == known:
            return name
    return "none"


def _app_keyvalues(data, strings, blob_start, entry_end):
    root, pos = _read_object(data, blob_start, entry_end, strings, 0, top=True)
    # Trailing document terminators, whose count has varied between versions.
    while pos < entry_end and data[pos] in (KV_END, KV_END_ALT, 0x00):
        pos += 1
    if pos != entry_end:
        raise ParseError(
            "an app entry ended %d bytes from where its own size said it would"
            % (entry_end - pos))
    return root


def load(path):
    """The whole cache, in one read, or a raise.

    ONE SHOT, ONE DESCRIPTOR. Valve's client rewrites this file whenever PICS
    tells it something changed, and a reader that returned to the disk between
    sections would splice two versions together. Reading it all at once means
    the worst case is a torn buffer, which the boundary checks above turn into
    a refusal rather than into a plausible wrong number.

    O_NONBLOCK, AND THE SIZE CHECKED THROUGH THE DESCRIPTOR WE OPENED. This
    path is inside the session user's home directory, so every component of it
    is something the unprivileged half of this machine can replace -- and this
    process is root. A plain open() of a name that had become a FIFO blocks
    until somebody writes to it, which on a single-threaded download loop is
    the unit sitting at `active (running)` forever with no download and nothing
    anywhere saying why; the contract names that trap for the request file and
    it is the same trap here. O_NONBLOCK returns from a FIFO immediately, and
    fstat on the descriptor (rather than stat on the name, which could be a
    different object by then) is what rejects it. A symlink pointing at
    somebody else's file is refused by the same check when it is not a regular
    file, and when it IS one the magic check refuses it a moment later --
    nothing read here is ever echoed anywhere.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise ParseError("cannot open %s (%s)" % (path, exc.strerror))
    try:
        info_ = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(info_.st_mode):
            raise ParseError("%s is not a regular file" % path)
        if info_.st_size > MAX_VDF_BYTES:
            raise ParseError(
                "%s is %d bytes, which is not a cache" % (path, info_.st_size))
        try:
            with os.fdopen(fd, "rb") as handle:
                fd = -1        # fdopen owns it now
                data = handle.read()
        except OSError as exc:
            raise ParseError("cannot read %s (%s)" % (path, exc.strerror))
    finally:
        if fd >= 0:
            os.close(fd)
    if not data:
        raise ParseError("%s is empty" % path)
    return data


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the sidecar says
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _sub(node, *names):
    """Walk a path of keys, returning None rather than raising at any miss."""
    for name in names:
        if not isinstance(node, dict):
            return None
        node = node.get(name)
    return node


def _text(value):
    """A KeyValues scalar as a string, or None when it is not a scalar.

    A dict is not a value: `installdir` arriving as an object would mean the
    path walked to something other than what it is named for, and turning that
    into `{...}` in a file steamdl makes a DIRECTORY NAME out of is how a
    download lands somewhere nobody meant.
    """
    if value is None or isinstance(value, dict):
        return None
    if isinstance(value, bool):
        return None
    if isinstance(value, int):
        return str(value)
    if isinstance(value, float):
        return None
    return str(value)


def _clean(value):
    """Safe to be a TSV field. See protocodec's module docstring for the bill.

    Tab is IFS whitespace to the shell, so a tab inside a game name would forge
    a column and shift every value after it one place left -- silently, into
    fields that all still look like plausible strings. That has broken two
    features in this tree already. Other C0 controls are dropped rather than
    escaped: a control character in a game name is not a thing worth preserving
    into a file another program parses.
    """
    out = []
    for char in value:
        code = ord(char)
        if char in ("\t", "\n"):
            out.append(" ")
        elif code < 0x20 or code == 0x7F:
            continue
        else:
            out.append(char)
    return "".join(out).strip()


def _app_section(root):
    """The section under which `common`, `config` and `depots` live.

    Each app's blob is one object named `appinfo` wrapping those sections, so
    the useful root is one level down -- but the wrapper's name is not
    something to depend on, so this looks for the sections themselves.
    """
    markers = ("common", "config", "depots")
    if isinstance(root, dict) and any(name in root for name in markers):
        return root
    if isinstance(root, dict):
        for value in root.values():
            if isinstance(value, dict) and any(name in value for name in markers):
                return value
    return root if isinstance(root, dict) else {}


def _native_linux(app):
    """1 only if some config.launch.<n>.config.oslist names linux.

    THE STORE-LEVEL common.oslist IS NOT USED, and that is the contract's
    instruction rather than a preference: it is a claim about what the title
    supports somewhere, while this is a question about whether THIS install has
    a runnable Linux entry point. Getting it wrong the permissive way means
    steamdl downloads a Linux depot for a game that has no Linux launcher and
    the person is left with a directory of files that cannot start.

    Returns "1", "0", or None for "there is no launch section, so we do not
    know" -- which is written as no row at all, and which steamdl reads as
    Windows-under-Proton. That is the right direction to be wrong in: a Windows
    build of a game that also ships a Linux build still runs here.
    """
    launch = _sub(app, "config", "launch")
    if not isinstance(launch, dict) or not launch:
        return None
    for entry in launch.values():
        if not isinstance(entry, dict):
            continue
        oslist = _text(_sub(entry, "config", "oslist"))
        if not oslist:
            continue
        for name in oslist.replace(";", ",").split(","):
            if name.strip().lower() == "linux":
                return "1"
    return "0"


def rows_for(app, branch):
    """The sidecar's rows, in the order steamdl documents them.

    Every row is omitted when its value is not in the cache. A missing row is
    an answer steamdl already handles and says out loud; a row holding a
    fallback would be the same shape as a real one and nothing downstream could
    tell them apart.
    """
    rows = []

    name = _text(_sub(app, "common", "name"))
    if name:
        rows.append(("name", name))

    installdir = _text(_sub(app, "config", "installdir"))
    if installdir:
        rows.append(("installdir", installdir))

    # THE BUILDID AND ITS BRANCH TRAVEL TOGETHER. The number means nothing
    # without the branch it came from, so the branch row is written only when
    # there is a buildid for it to label -- a `branch public` row beside no
    # buildid would be a claim about nothing.
    buildid = _text(_sub(app, "depots", "branches", branch, "buildid"))
    if buildid and buildid.isdigit():
        rows.append(("branch", branch))
        rows.append(("buildid", buildid))

    native = _native_linux(app)
    if native is not None:
        rows.append(("native_linux", native))

    # Informational, per the shape steamdl's own header documents. Nothing
    # reads them today; they are what makes a sidecar answerable by eye when
    # somebody is working out why an install did not match.
    depots = _sub(app, "depots")
    if isinstance(depots, dict):
        for key in sorted(depots, key=lambda k: (len(k), k)):
            if key.isdigit():
                rows.append(("depot", key))

    return [(key, _clean(value)) for key, value in rows if _clean(value)]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Writing it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def write_sidecar(appid, rows):
    """TEMP-THEN-RENAME, 0644, explicitly chmodded.

    The rename is for the reason every other seam in this feature has one:
    steamdl reads these files at the top of an install and would otherwise be
    able to read one inside a truncating open() -- a game installed with the
    buildid of nothing. The chmod is because the umask of whatever unit invoked
    this is not something to trust for a file another uid may later need.
    """
    try:
        os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, 0o755)
    except FileExistsError:
        pass
    except OSError as exc:
        err("cannot create %s (%s)" % (OUT_DIR, exc.strerror))
        return False

    final = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, "%d.tsv" % appid)
    temp = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, ".%d.%d.tmp" % (appid, os.getpid()))
    body = "".join("%s\t%s\n" % (key, value) for key, value in rows)
    try:
        with open(temp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write(body)
        os.chmod(temp, 0o644)
        os.replace(temp, final)
    except OSError as exc:
        err("cannot write %s (%s)" % (final, exc.strerror))
        try:
            os.unlink(temp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return False
    return True


def main():
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    path = CLIENT_APPINFO
    branch = DEFAULT_BRANCH
    to_stdout = False
    appids = []

    while argv:
        arg = argv.pop(0)
        if arg == "--print":
            to_stdout = True
        elif arg == "--vdf" and argv:
            path = argv.pop(0)
        elif arg == "--branch" and argv:
            branch = argv.pop(0)
        elif arg.isdigit() and len(arg) <= 10:
            appids.append(int(arg))
        else:
            err("usage: appinfo [--print] [--vdf PATH] [--branch NAME] <appid>...")
            return 2
    if not appids:
        err("no appid given")
        return 2

    if not os.path.exists(path):
        # THE NORMAL CASE ON A NEW MACHINE, and not an error. The client writes
        # this cache the first time it runs; until then there is nothing to
        # read and steamdl already says what that costs.
        warn("no %s yet -- the client has not run on this machine" % path)
        warn("installs will proceed with no buildid and a guessed installdir")
        return 1

    try:
        data = load(path)
        magic, strings, apps = _index(data)
    except ParseError as exc:
        # Everything reaches here: a torn read while the client rewrites the
        # file, a Steam-side format change, a truncated cache. All of them mean
        # the same thing and get the same answer -- no sidecar.
        err("cannot read %s: %s" % (path, exc))
        err("no sidecar written; steamdl will install with buildid 0 and say so")
        return 1

    info("%s: magic 0x%08x, %d apps%s" % (
        path, magic, len(apps),
        ", %d strings" % len(strings) if strings is not None else ""))

    failed = 0
    for appid in appids:
        if appid not in apps:
            warn("app %d is not in the client's cache" % appid)
            failed += 1
            continue
        blob_start, entry_end, hashes = apps[appid]
        try:
            root = _app_keyvalues(data, strings, blob_start, entry_end)
        except ParseError as exc:
            err("cannot parse app %d: %s" % (appid, exc))
            failed += 1
            continue
        info("app %d: blob sha1 %s" % (
            appid, _blob_sha1(data, blob_start, entry_end, hashes)))

        rows = rows_for(_app_section(root), branch)
        if not rows:
            warn("app %d is in the cache but says nothing we can use" % appid)
            failed += 1
            continue

        if to_stdout:
            for key, value in rows:
                sys.stdout.write("%s\t%s\n" % (key, value))
            continue

        if not write_sidecar(appid, rows):
            failed += 1
            continue
        keys = [key for key, _ in rows]
        if "buildid" not in keys:
            # Named at warning level because this is the one absence whose
            # consequence is a second download of a game that is already here.
            warn("app %d has no %s buildid in the cache" % (appid, branch))
        info("app %d: %s" % (appid, " ".join(keys)))

    return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
